um i think one of the pieces that is so  interesting to me personally is  uh not only did you scale uh what was a  family business into this large national  chain  uh but you also were uh consistently  tapped for  your experience and knowledge so you sat  on the boards of domino's and sonic and  kind of these other types of businesses  and as you think back how much of  what you were learning as an operator uh  at  dunkin donuts gave you the ability to  kind of be a good board member be a good  sounding board and governor  of those other businesses right were  there a lot of kind of  um common lessons and common uh  challenges and obstacles that each of  these businesses were going through  or did you find that um even though they  were kind of in the food services space  these other businesses actually had  different challenges and different  things that they dealt with than  what you were dealing with as the  operator seat at duncan  well 35 years is a long run and it gave  me an opportunity to  make a lot of mistakes and  so those setbacks and successes i think  were helpful  as i served on other boards and  basically i  was a believer that i you know stick  stuck to my knitting  to things i knew so i only served on  boards or either  of uh chain operators small box  retailers i call them they either  utilize franchise  as a method of distribution and it could  have been in the automotive industry  or or it could have been in the food  service industry and  and i did find uh that on  on a board that there is an advantage to  having  an operator alongside other types  people who understand governance people  who understand maybe international  marketing what other issues that  business may be facing i think comprise  an effective award  but having a practitioner someone who's  been there done that's been helpful and  the answer to your question is yes  i found my experiences were  extraordinarily helpful  uh for example i was on one board uh  where we owned a  cotton uh a muffler repair and  non-muffled repair but the transmission  repair business  and we bought amco well i had experience  and  buying my competitor mr donut and  rebadging them to duncan  so that kind of experience could have  been very very useful  to to us as cotman as we bought amco  and merged the two brands together and  began to rebadge the stores under one  brand  so there were lots and lots of  experiences that i think i brought from  one  business even to the automotive  aftermarket repair business  that were useful absolutely and so  obviously a lot of what you've kind of  learned and i think built your career on  uh was finding the best people right and  kind of just whether it was uh  other board members uh executives  franchisees  just people people people when you  thought about people how much of this  was trying to find kind of  talent that was uh impressive but did  not yet  have kind of years and years of  experience doing something and so they  could kind of grow into the role  versus you wanted to go find people who  had already done the job let's say at  another company or on a bigger scale  like were you hiring for uh experience  or were you  more focused on hiring for kind of  potential and  competency there were three things that  i  utilized really the company utilized  the first was a real crisp  understanding of the assignment we  wanted somewhat to fill  and the better you define the assignment  the more likely you are to find a  candidate to come fulfill that  assignment  so i spent a lot of time thinking about  what is it  exactly where are we what do we need  filled in that job  so it's a definition of a job the second  philosophy or principle that we sort of  followed was  that we weren't looking for someone who  was  uh effective or knowledgeable in all  things we really really  we were a team culture we relied on  the complementarity of the skills in the  team  and it was no big including myself  no big admission not to be good at  everything certain things  we were thrown to be better at and not  so good at others  and the whole notion was to have in the  team the complementarity of skills  that we really would support and lift  each other up so long as you had mutual  respect  no backbiting which was not allowed in  our culture we  basically wouldn't put up with that and  the third thing was fit  and you had to fit the culture and the  culture in our case was  you know a teamwork aspirations yet  we were we were we liked to win uh  winning was a part of us just dna  so whenever we sort of achieved one  objective we set more lofty objectives  to  to spur us forward we we kept changing  all the time and kept growing  so i would say those were the three sort  of principles  that we utilized in some cases uh it  didn't require a lot of prior knowledge  and  in fact just the fit and the  complementarity of  the personalities might have been enough  depending on the assignment  yeah it's really interesting how um  you obviously did this for so long you  know exactly what you were looking for  and i think that that's a key  piece to this is uh if you just go out  and say hey we want to hire somebody you  obviously can make all kinds of mistakes  but when you say we specifically want to  hire somebody based on these three  criteria  that focus seems to drastically increase  the probability that you do it the right  way right  i agree absolutely
 

